  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Widespread rain gradually ends this morning. Rain\nwill be heavy at times southeast MA and RI, where flood risk is\ngreatest. Another round of lighter showers this afternoon into\nSunday.\n\nBroad low pressure along a stalled front across eastern MA and\nRI will slowly move offshore this afternoon and evening. This \nlow pressure should finally reach Downeast Maine and Nova Scotia\naround daybreak Sunday.\n\nWhile this means the widespread rainfall should be offshore by\nthis afternoon, we will still have to contend with a rather\npotent mid level low. This low should pass overhead tonight. The\ncolder air aloft will result in increased lapse rates, which in\nturn should generate some showers later today and tonight. \nThese showers will be much lighter in intensity. The greatest \ncoverage of showers should be towards northern MA. \n\nAbove normal temperatures continue yet again today, but will be\ntrending closer to normal. Expecting only slightly above normal\ntemperatures for Sunday. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Cooler and drier conditions arrive next week,\nthough it could turn unsettled once more towards the second \nhalf of the week.\n\nColder air aloft continues to shift into southern New England\nfor the start of next week as yet another upper level trough \ntracks into the region. This is a bit more notable at 850 mb and\n500 mb, where temperatures fall to just below 0C and to around \n-24C at those respective levels. With cooler air aloft and a \npassing shortwave just to our north, we could see some showers \naround Monday. 925 mb temperatures fall to around 2-4C by Monday\nnight, supporting lows falling further into the 30s and low \n40s, with highs Tuesday and the start of the week generally in \nthe 50s to low 60s. Cooler nights will persist through the week.\n\nNAEFS and other ensemble guidance indicate no significant\ndeviations from the climatological percentiles for temperatures \nor moisture for this coming week, though did note 90th\npercentile zonal winds aloft Monday into Wednesday just to the\nsouth of the region. Persistent westerly flow with notably\nbreezy conditions Monday and Tuesday is expected. Winds\ngenerally stay westerly through much of the week, given the\nseries of fronts expected to move through the region. With the\ndrier conditions (PWATS to around 0.5\" and less and dew points \nback in the 30s and 40s) at the surface, not much in the way of \nprecipitation is expected with these frontal passages; just \ncooler air. Occasional cloudiness is expected at most with \npassing moist air aloft.\n\nTowards the second half of next week, guidance has started to\npick up on the possible development of another low pressure\noriginating from just to the south of the region along/just off \nthe mid- Atlantic coast. This looks to be driven by the approach\nof an upper level shortwave tracking from Canada southeast into\nthe mid-Atlantic region. With this possible system, we could see \nthe return of more unsettled conditions for the second half of \nthe week.",
